    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d322">

                <pb facs="0258" n="254" xml:id="pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">124.932/569: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Third Secretary of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Allison</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-02T16:00:00+08:00">February 2, 1938—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 2—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>46. Embassy’s February 1, noon,<anchor n="44" xml:id="d322-f1"/> and January 24,
                    4 p.m.<anchor n="45" xml:id="d322-f2"/> Due to press of business Mr. Fukui was
                    unable to see me to discuss the Department’s instructions until just recently.
                    With regard to point 1 he stated it would be necessary to consult higher
                    authorities before stating what the Japanese Government would do. He asked me
                    why the State Department did not wish to have the apology made through the local
                    diplomatic channels and insisted upon it being made by a military officer. I
                    replied that I had no information on that point.</p>
                <p>In regard to point 3, Mr. Fukui again stated that the Japanese Government could
                    not be responsible for damages resulting from military operations and said that
                    if this was insisted upon it would be necessary to handle the claims in Tokyo.
                    He suggested that the matter of violation of Embassy property be settled first
                    and that we then discuss the settlement of private claims. I told him that if
                    the first matter could be settled promptly, I saw no objection to this
                    procedure. Mr. Fukui promised to call upon me shortly and give the Japanese
                    answer to the Department’s demands, but he has not yet done so. I intend to see
                    him this evening and ask when I may expect his reply.</p>
                <p>Sent to Embassy, Hankow, repeated to Peiping and Shanghai. Shanghai please repeat
                    to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Allison</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
